      "filePath": "/tmp/iss-ue-ca-plugin/claude/uac/ue-ca/skills/retrieving-context/references/ue-architect-notes.md",
      "content": "# Universal Extension Architecture Guide\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Core Entities and Workflow](#core-entities-and-workflow)\n   1. [Entity Hierarchy](#entity-hierarchy)\n   2. [Workflow](#workflow)\n2. [Universal Extension Components](#universal-extension-components)\n3. [Execution Environment](#execution-environment)\n4. [Input Configuration](#input-configuration)\n   1. [Common Input Fields](#common-input-fields)\n      1. [Recommendations for Environment Variables](#recommendations-for-environment-variables)\n      2. [Recommendations for Log Level](#recommendations-for-log-level)\n   2. [UAC Extension-Specific Input Fields](#uac-extension-specific-input-fields)\n      1. [Text Field](#text-field)\n      2. [Integer Field](#integer-field)\n      3. [Float Field](#float-field)\n      4. [Boolean Field](#boolean-field)\n      5. [Choice Fields](#choice-fields)\n         1. [Standard Choice Fields](#standard-choice-fields)\n         2. [Dynamic Choice Fields](#dynamic-choice-fields)\n      6. [Credential Field](#credential-field)\n      7. [Script Field](#script-field)\n      8. [Array Field](#array-field)\n5. [Field Behavior](#field-behavior)\n6. [Output Types](#output-types)\n   1. [Execution Results](#execution-results)\n      1. [Return Code](#return-code)\n      2. [Status Description](#status-description)\n   2. [Runtime Outputs](#runtime-outputs)\n      1. [General Recommendations for Runtime Outputs](#general-recommendations-for-runtime-outputs)\n   3. [Terminal State Output](#terminal-state-output)\n7. [Task Instance Lifecycle](#task-instance-lifecycle)\n   1. [Task Instance Status/States](#task-instance-statusstates)\n   2. [Task Instance Commands](#task-instance-commands)\n      1. [Launch](#launch)\n      2. [Cancel](#cancel)\n      3. [Dynamic Command](#dynamic-command)\n      4. [Re-run](#re-run)\n8. [Data Persistence and Caching](#data-persistence-and-caching)\n   1. [Persistence](#persistence)\n   2. [Caching on Agent's local filesystem](#caching-on-agents-local-filesystem)\n9. [Design Patterns](#design-patterns)\n   1. [Action Dispatcher Pattern](#action-dispatcher-pattern)\n   2. [Resource Identifier Persistence Pattern](#resource-identifier-persistence-pattern)\n   3. [Multi-Channel Output Pattern](#multi-channel-output-pattern)\n   4. [Partial Success / Error Behavior Pattern](#partial-success--error-behavior-pattern)\n   5. [Temporary File Handling Pattern](#temporary-file-handling-pattern)\n   6. [Output Verbosity Selection Pattern](#output-verbosity-selection-pattern)\n   7. [Large Output Safety Net Pattern](#large-output-safety-net-pattern)\n10. [Start Small](#start-small)\n11. [Field Organization Strategies](#field-organization-strategies)\n12. [Python Modules selection framework](#python-modules-selection-framework)\n13. [Python Modules specific Notes](#python-modules-specific-notes)\n   1. [HTTPX vs REQUESTS](#httpx-vs-requests)\n   2. [Tabulate module (or similar ones)](#tabulate-module-or-similar-ones)\n14. [Related Documentation](#related-documentation)\n\n---\n\n## Core Entities and Workflow\n\n### Entity Hierarchy\n- **Universal Extension**: Defines task capability\n- **Task Definition**: Blueprint for creating executable UAC Task Instances\n- **Task Instance**: Executable Python code that runs on UAC Agents\n\n### Workflow\n```\nUniversal Extension \u2192 Task Definition \u2192 Task Instance (executable on the Universal Agent)\n```\n\n## Universal Extension Components\n\nA Universal Extension consists of three parts:\n\n1. **Universal Template**: Defines fields, UI components, and high-level behavior\n2. **Extension Code**: Implements:\n   - Task instance execution logic\n   - Dynamic choice field population\n   - Dynamic command execution\n   - Cancellation logic\n3. **Icon**: Displayed in the UAC UI\n\n- All 3 items are bundled in a so called extension archive (zip file).\n- Dependent python modules are bundled inside the extension archive during build time.\n- It is possible to bundle pure-Python modules as well as Python modules with C bindings. This allows direct extension deployment through Universal Agent without the need of manual installation of python module dependencies at the Agent Host.\n\n### Platform Compatibility and Dependency Bundling\n\nThe build system (`setup.py`) enforces platform-specific constraints during dependency installation:\n\n- **On Linux build machines**: pip is invoked with `--only-binary=:all:` and `--platform=manylinux_2_17_x86_64`. This means pip will not compile from source. Pure-Python modules (universal `py3-none-any` wheels) are always accepted. C extension modules require a `manylinux_2_17_x86_64` binary wheel \u2014 if none exists, the build **hard fails**.\n- **On Windows build machines**: no platform constraint is applied. pip installs Windows-specific wheels or compiles from source, producing Windows binaries that will not run on Linux UAC agents.\n- A single build run using only pure-Python modules produces a platform-agnostic bundle compatible with both Linux and Windows. However, if any C extension module is included, the resulting bundle is platform-specific and will not run on a different platform.\n\n**Consequences for module selection based on target UAC agent platform:**\n\n| Target Platform | C Extension Module | Viability |\n|---|---|---|\n| Linux-only | Has `manylinux_2_17_x86_64` wheel | Viable |\n| Linux-only | No `manylinux_2_17_x86_64` wheel | Not viable \u2014 build hard fails |\n| Windows-only | Any C extension | Not viable when building on Linux |\n| Cross-platform (both) | Any C extension | Not viable \u2014 single build cannot serve both platforms |\n| Any | Pure-Python | Always viable \u2014 cross-platform, no binary constraints |\n\n**Key principle**: When the target platform is Windows-only, cross-platform, or not explicitly stated, only pure-Python modules are safe choices. Linux-only targets are the most permissive \u2014 they unlock C extension modules with `manylinux_2_17` wheels and generally provide access to higher-performance libraries.\n\n\n\n## Execution Environment\n\n- **Runtime**: Task instances execute on UAC Agents\n- **Runtime Directory**: Working directory on the Agent where task instance executes\n- **Runtime Context**: \n  - Extension has access to all field values from input and output fields (output fields from an previous execution of task instance)\n  - Extension has access to all Universal Controller variables visible in the task instance variable scope \n  - UAC Scripts (Data Scripts) are referenced through transient filesystam paths. Before the task instance is spawed as a python process, the Agent gets the UAC Script , places it temporary on the filesystem. The Task Instance is aware of the filesystem path of the file and therefore it can use it.\n\n## Input Configuration\n\n### Common Input Fields\nAvailable for all tasks without having to be per-se configured. They have the same functionality for all extensions\n\n- **Agent**: Target Agent for task instance execution\n- **Runtime Directory**: Execution directory on the Agent\n- **Environment Variables**: Configured at Task Definition level, available during execution\n- **Log Level**: Logger sensitivity for STDERR (INFO, DEBUG, TRACE, etc.)\n\n#### Recommendations for Environment Variables\n\n- Be in favor of environment variables for input parameters that are not commonly tuned and/or have some sensible defaults like HTTP Client Timeouts (UE_HTTP_TIMEOUT)\n- Environment variable name selection: \n  - Do not propose new environment variables when there are already well established environment variables for functionalities supported by the selected python modules \n  - When you need to propose a new environemnt variable, prefix the name with \"UE_\" \n\n#### Recommendations for Log Level\n\n- It is not required to create a new field to configure the Log Level as it is already provided out of the box.\n\n\n### UAC Extension-Specific Input Fields\n\n**Configuration Philosophy**: \n- There is No global configuration that applies the same configuration for all the Tasks that belong to a specific extension\n- However Extension fields can have default values (Sensible Defaults) that are provided during task definition time\n\nThe following core Field Types exist for an extension\n\n### Text Field\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Text Field 1-20\"` or `\"Large Text Field 1-4\"`\n**When to Use:**\n- Text Field: Small URLs, hostnames, email addresses, names\n- Large Text (can be multiline as well): Descriptions, notes, formatted data, potentially Large URLs\n**Usual Practice:** Use Text Field for most string inputs; use Large Text Field for longer content\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `str | None` - String value or None if empty/not provided\n\n---\n\n### Integer Field\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Integer Field 1-10\"`\n**When to Use:** Whole number values (ports, timeouts, counts, IDs)\n**Usual Practice:** Include `intFieldMin` and `intFieldMax` to constrain valid ranges\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `int | None` - Integer value or None if empty/not provided\n\n---\n\n### Float Field\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Float Field 1-4\"`\n**When to Use:** Decimal number values (percentages, rates, measurements)\n**Usual Practice:** Rarely used; prefer Integer when possible\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `float | None` - Float value or None if empty/not provided\n\n---\n\n### Boolean Field\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Boolean Field 1-15\"`\n**When to Use:** Yes/no toggles, enable/disable switches, Show/Hide \"Advanced Usage\" fields\n**Usual Practice:** Use for simple true/false choices; use Choice field for multiple options\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `bool` - Always returns True or False based on booleanYesValue/booleanNoValue\n\n---\n\n### Choice Fields\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Choice Field 1-20\"`\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `list[str]` - Always returns an array:\n- Empty selection (when `choiceAllowEmpty=true`): `[]`\n- Single selection: `[\"selected_value\"]`\n- Multi selection (when `choiceAllowMultiple=true`): `[\"opt1\", \"opt2\"]`\n\n#### Standard Choice Fields\n- **Purpose:** Single or multi choice dropdown selections that are retrieved from the template definition\n- **When to Use:** For fixed selections \n- **Usual Practice:** Use for any field with fixed options (actions, modes, types)\n- **Structure**: Label (user-friendly, descriptive) + Value (passed to extension code)\n- **Validation**: Choice field values do not require validation at code level as they are predefined on the template\n- **UX**: Using choice fields, helps to avoid typing errors, or invalid selections\n- **Restriction**: Values MUST NOT contain commas\n\n\n#### Dynamic Choice Fields\n\n- **Purpose:** Single or multi choice dynamic dropdown of selections that are retrieved programmatically\n- **use Cases:**\n  - Choices can be retrieved from remote services (e.g. by calling an endpoint, or a python method)\n  - Choices can be retrieved from static list, dictionaries defined at code level\n  - Choices can be retrieved from Data Scripts as python code has physical access to the them (Universal Controller sends scripts to Universal Agents and Agents store them temporarily on the filesystem. Extensions know the temporary path of those scripts)\n  - Conditional visibility based on other field values like (like, when country field value is X, the Cities field values are Y1, Y2, Y3).\n- **Purpose**: Choices retrieved programmatically\n- **Input**: Other field values\n- **Output**: Field choices for user selection\n- **UX**: Using choice fields, helps to avoid typing errors, or invalid selections\n- **Restriction**: Values MUST NOT contain commas\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Credential Field\n\nCredentials are specific entities within UAC and are referenced through Credential Fields.\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Credential Field 1-6\"`\n**Purpose:** Secure authentication data passing\n**When to Use:** username, password, token, key location (for SFTP, or other enctyption key paths), passphrase\n**Usual Practice:** Use for any sensitive authentication data\n**UAC Credential Mapping Policy**:\n  - DO: Map each credential value to exactly one UAC Credential attribute\n  - DO: Map API keys to `password` attribute. If the length of the API key is more than 255 characters, map it ot `token` attribute.\n  - DO: Treat the selected attribute as the single source of truth\n  - DO: `user` attribute is always mandatory for the definition of a credential entity, even though it migh be not used for the business logic\n  - DON'T: Use `user` attribute for sensitive information\n  - DON'T: Implement fallback logic (e.g., \"check token, if empty check password\")\n  - DON'T: Query multiple attributes for the same credential value\n\n\n**Credential Field Strategy**\n\nUse Single Shared Credential When:\n- All actions use same authentication\n- Extension targets single system/API\n\nUse Action-Specific Credentials When:\n- Different actions require different permission levels\n- Some actions are read-only, others need admin access\n- Multiple systems involved (read from A, write to B)\n\n**Other Suggestions**\n\n- Configure Credentials to be used also as variables as it provides maximum flexibility \n  \n**Returns To Extension Code:** `dict | None` - Returns dictionary with credential keys/attributes (only `user` is required), or None if credential is not provided:\n```python\n{\n    \"user\": str,  # Required\n    \"password\": str | None,\n    \"token\": str | None,\n    \"passphrase\": str | None,\n    \"key_location\": str | None,\n    \"keyLocation\": str | None\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Script Field\n\nScripts are specific entities within UAC and are referenced through Script Fields. The Scripts that are used in Universal Extensions are always of type \"Data Script\".\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Script Field 1-4\"`\n**When to Use:** \n- Payloads (Like JSON/XML/SQL etc)\n- Configuration options (like configparser)\n**Usual Practice:** Use for complex user defined data that require structure, hierarchy, re-usability  \n**Returns To Extension Code:** `str | None` - Returns a temporary file path containing the script content on the Agent's filesystem. Extension must read the file to access content, or None if empty/not provided\n\n\n---\n\n### Array Field\n\n**Mapping:** `\"Array Field 1-4\"`\n**When to Use:** Key-value pairs, headers, parameters\n**Usual Practice:** Use for variable-length lists of name-value pairs. \n**User Interface**: From User Interface perspective it is a table with two columns (name,value) of configurable title and user can create row items and populate the column values as text. Example: If we had to map HTTP header name and value into an array field,the name title could be \"Header Name\" and value title would be \"Header Value\"\n**Returns To Extension Code:** `list[dict] | None` - Returns list of dictionaries with `\"name\"` and `\"value\"` keys, or None if empty:\n```python\n[\n    {\"name\": \"key1\", \"value\": \"value1\"},\n    {\"name\": \"key2\", \"value\": \"value2\"}\n]\n```\n**Recommendation**: Do not propose Array Fields for simple lists. Propose only when you have key value pairs as it might consuse users.\n---\n\n## Field Behavior\n\n- **Default Values**: Set at template level (not required in extension code)\n- **Validations**: Validation that are set on the Template level such as Length, Regex for text fields\n- **Conditional Visibility/Requirement**: Fields can be shown/hidden or be required based on other field values\n  - **rules**\n    - A field can depend **on one and only one** field to be shown or be required.\n    - It is not possible to have cyclic dependencies (like: a field A depending on visibility on field B and field B depending on visibility on field A)\n    - Mode \"Require If\": Require a Field A to have a value if Field B value(s) IN (X,Y), is a supported functionality.\n    - Mode \"Show IF\": Show Field A if Field B value(s) IN (X,Y) is a supported functionality. In this case flag \"Required\" for the Field A can be set (which means if Shown then it is also required)\n    - Mode \"Require IF\" and \"Show If\" are mutually exclusive (cannot be used at the same time)\n  - **suggestion**: Since visibility of a field cannot depend on more than one field it is possible that users can populate or choose incompatible choices. In this case you have two options.\n    - Either combine two or more fields into one, if this make sense from User Experience point of view\n    - Or, do validation at code level, before the execution of the actuall business logic. In this case you allow the user to potentially populate incompatible values during task definition time, and validation is during run-time. \n- **Field Metadata**:\n  - **Label**: Field name displayed in UI (very important for User Experience)\n  - **Hint**: Help text (maximum 200 characters)\n\n## Output Types\n\n### Execution Results\n\n#### Return Code \n\n**Recommendation**: Simplest approach and best practice for most extensions:\n  - `0`: Successful execution\n  - `1`: Failed execution (covers all error scenarios appart from validation errors)\n  - `20`: Validation Errors\n\n#### Status Description \n\n- Short text describing success or error scenario\n- **Recommendation**: The following format is usually the best for most cases `<Error Category>: <Error Description>`. \n- **Examples:**\n\n``` \n  - `Success: Job was executed successfully`\n  - `Authentication Error: Invalid Credentials`\n  - `Connection Error: Hostname invalid.host.com could not be resolved`\n  - `Data Validation Error: Input Data for field email_address invalid.` \n```\n\n### Runtime Outputs\nPropagated during task instance execution providing immediate and very useful information to users.\n\n- **Output Only Fields**: Specific field-based information displayed in UI\n  - **Recommendations**: \n    - 2-3 fields are suitable for most cases. Choose the most important information to be displayed for best User Experience\n    - Do not store large Information on Output Only fields. Use short information directly visible and understood by users.  \n- **Progress Bar**: Measurable progress (percentage format)\n  - Not important for short-lived tasks or non-measurable operations\n- **STDERR**: \n  - Standard logging with different log levels\n  - Displayed on UC UI\n  - Log level is controlled by starndard field \"Log Level\" standard common input field\n  - **Recommendations**: Use detailed information, error messages and stack traces, then task instance is started in log Level DEBUG\n- **STDOUT**: \n  - Human-readable business logic information.\n  - Displayed on UC UI \n  - **Recommendations**: \n    - ASCII Table format is preferable when information can be printed nicely in rows or in columns using well known python libraries\n    - See Design Patterns section: `Output Verbosity Selection Pattern` and `Large Output Safety Net Pattern`\n\n#### General Recommendations for Runtime Outputs\n\n- Use simple text to avoid encoding errors (for example, for temperature symbol Celcious use,  \"22.5 C\" (not \"22.5\u00b0C\"))\n\n### Terminal State Output\n\n**EXTENSION OUTPUT**: \n  - Machine-processable output (JSON format)\n  - Available only when task reaches terminal state (Success/Failure/Cancellation)\n\n**Recommendations**:\n- Always provide an Extension Output\n- In case of failure scenarios it is recommended to use an `error` object to include metadata and description about the error\n- Enclose output information in a `result` object\n- See Design Patterns section: `Output Verbosity Selection Pattern` and `Large Output Safety Net Pattern`\n\n## Task Instance Lifecycle\n\n### Task Instance Status/States\n\nThe most important states for a task instance are the following: \n\n- **Status**: Running, **Trigger**: Launch action\n- **Status**: Success, **Trigger**: Successful Completion\n- **Status**: Failed, **Trigger**: Error/failure condition\n- **Status**: Cancelled, **Trigger**:  Cancel action\n\n\n### Task Instance Commands\n\n#### Launch\n- Available by default\n- Starts task instance execution\n\n#### Cancel\n- Available by default\n- **Default Behavior**: \n  - Sends TERM signal to process. \n  - TERM signal should not be caught, as for specific implementation the \"Custom Logic\" scenario should be used.\n  - No special code implmentation is required and no specific handling is covered by the extension code.\n- **Custom Logic**: \n  - Implemented in extension code to fulfil a specific clean-up operation\n- **Additional Notes**:\n  - In case there is no specific cancellation logic mentioned on the requirements, no code should be implemented the default behavior is applied.\n\n#### Dynamic Command\n- **Description**: Programmable runtime command invoked from Universal Controller UI for a specific Task instance\n- **Definition**:\n  - Defined first on the Universal Template of the extension\n  - The business logic, is defined in the universal extension source code.\n- **Conditions for execution**: \n  - Can be configured to be invoked only on specific Task Instance States (like \"Running\")\n\n#### Re-run\n- **Availability**: For failed/canceled task instances\n- **Previous Run Data**: Output Only fields from previous run are accessible\n- **Use Case**: Extension code can use previous run data for specific business logic\n- **Additional Notes**: Inside the Extension code there is no explicit way to know if a task instance execution is a re-run or not. The only way this can be known is only implicitly. If there are some output only fields populated then it is clear that this is a Re-Run, since output only fields can only be populated from previous runs. If a specific re-run logic exists, then output only fields should be evaluated from previous run and used in the business logic of the Re-Run. Else a Re-Run would behave like an normal execution.\n\n## Data Persistence and Caching\n\n### Persistence\n- **Method**: Local files on Agent file system (simplest form)\n- **Use Case**: Store data for subsequent task instances within a UAC workflow\n\n### Caching on Agent's local filesystem\n- **Recommendation**: NOT recommended\n- **Reason**: \n  - Task instances may execute on different agents, making cache unreliable.\n\n\n## Design Patterns\n\n### Action Dispatcher Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** Extensions supporting multiple operations (Create, Update, Delete, Get Status)\n\n**Structure:**\n- Choice field for action selection at top of form\n- Conditional field groups for each action type\n- Each action shows only relevant fields using `showIfField`\n\n**Example Structure:**\n```json\n{\n  \"fields\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"action_type\",\n      \"fieldMapping\": \"Choice Field 1\",\n      \"choices\": [\n        {\"fieldValue\": \"Create\"},\n        {\"fieldValue\": \"Update\"},\n        {\"fieldValue\": \"Delete\"},\n        {\"fieldValue\": \"GetStatus\"}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"resource_name\",\n      \"fieldMapping\": \"Text Field 1\",\n      \"showIfField\": \"Choice Field 1\",\n      \"showIfFieldValue\": \"Create\",\n      \"requireIfVisible\": true,\n      \"noSpaceIfHidden\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"resource_id\",\n      \"fieldMapping\": \"Text Field 2\",\n      \"showIfField\": \"Choice Field 1\",\n      \"showIfFieldValue\": \"Update,Delete,GetStatus\",\n      \"requireIfVisible\": true,\n      \"noSpaceIfHidden\": true\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Resource Identifier Persistence Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** When you want to persist Ids, or other information that is required for future operations (Re-Run or Dynamic Command operations)\n\n**Structure:**\n- Output-only field for storing created resource ID\n- Set `fieldRestriction: \"Output Only\"` and `extensionStatus: true`\n- Use `preserveOutputOnRerun: true` to retain ID across task reruns\n- User can reference output field value in subsequent tasks\n\n**Example:**\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"created_resource_id\",\n  \"fieldMapping\": \"Text Field 10\",\n  \"label\": \"Created Resource ID\",\n  \"fieldRestriction\": \"Output Only\",\n  \"extensionStatus\": true,\n  \"preserveOutputOnRerun\": true,\n  \"defaultListView\": true\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Channel Output Pattern\n\n**Scenario:** Extension needs to provide different types of output for different consumption scenarios.\n\n**Understanding Output Channels:**\n1. **Output Fields** - Visible in UAC UI, populated by extension setting `output_data.field_name`\n2. **STDOUT** - Everything printed by extension (`print()` statements)\n3. **Extension Output** - JSON object returned by extension containing `exit_code`, `status_description`, `invocation`, and `result`\n\n**Approach:**\n- **Status Description Field** (Output Field): Quick human-readable status in UI (\"Success\", \"Failed\", \"Completed 5/10 items\")\n- **Detail Output Field** (Output Field): Full execution information visible in task details\n- **STDOUT**: Real-time execution logs for debugging\n- **Extension Output**: Complete structured data including invocation details and results for automation/auditing\n\n**Example Template Fields:**\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"status\",\n    \"fieldMapping\": \"Text Field 10\",\n    \"label\": \"Status\",\n    \"fieldRestriction\": \"Output Only\",\n    \"extensionStatus\": true,\n    \"defaultListView\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"response_data\",\n    \"fieldMapping\": \"Large Text Field 1\",\n    \"label\": \"Response Data\",\n    \"fieldRestriction\": \"Output Only\",\n    \"extensionStatus\": true\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**Example Extension Output JSON:**\n```json\n{\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"status_description\": \"AI response generated successfully\",\n  \"invocation\": {\n    \"extension\": \"ue-azure-openai\",\n    \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n    \"fields\": {\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI\",\n      \"model\": \"gpt-4o\",\n      \"temperature\": 1.0\n    }\n  },\n  \"result\": {\n    \"response\": \"2 + 2 equals 4.\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n      \"prompt_tokens\": 25,\n      \"completion_tokens\": 9,\n      \"total_tokens\": 34\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Usage Strategy:**\n- **Output Fields**: Populate with key information users need to see in UI\n- **STDOUT**: Print execution progress and debugging information\n- **Extension Output**: Return complete structured data for downstream processing and audit trails\n- **Status Description**: Automatically captured by UAC, appears in task execution status\n\n---\n\n### Partial Success / Error Behavior Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** Extension processes multiple items (files, records, messages) and the user needs control over whether to stop on the first error or continue processing all items. It is a suggested approach.\n\n**Structure:**\n- Choice field `error_behavior` with values `\"Fail Fast\"` and `\"Continue\"`\n- Use exit code `2` for partial success (some items processed, some failed) \u2014 unless `2` is already used for another purpose\n- Status description summarizes counts: `\"Processed 3/5 files \u2014 2 failures\"`\n\n**Exit Code Convention:**\n- `0`: All items processed successfully\n- `1`: Execution failed (includes Fail Fast scenario \u2014 stops on the first error)\n- `2`: Partial success \u2014 Continue mode completed but at least one item failed\n- `20`: Validation error\n\n---\n\n### Temporary File Handling Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** Extension needs to create temporary files or directories during execution (e.g., intermediate processing, format conversion, staging data).\n\n**Recommended Approach:** Use Python's built-in `tempfile` module (`NamedTemporaryFile`, `TemporaryDirectory`) as context managers. Temporary files are automatically deleted on context exit, including when exceptions occur. No third-party dependency is needed.\n\n**Key Notes:**\n- Always use the `with` statement to guarantee cleanup\n- Use `NamedTemporaryFile` for a single temporary file\n- Use `TemporaryDirectory` for multiple files or a directory structure\n- Automatic cleanup is guaranteed for normal exits and exceptions\n- Cleanup does **not** occur for SIGTERM (default Cancel) or SIGKILL \u2014 temp files will be left behind\n- When a custom cancellation routine is implemented, it can either explicitly clean up temp files or allow the extension to exit gracefully, letting the context manager automatic cleanup apply\n\n---\n\n### Output Verbosity Selection Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** Extension produces STDOUT and/or Extension Output where the content or volume may not always be needed, and the user should control what is included at task definition time.\n\n**Structure:**\n- If the total number of output options across both STDOUT and Extension Output is two or fewer, use a single Choice Field named `\"Output Options\"`\n- If either STDOUT or Extension Output has more than two distinct options, use two separate Choice Fields: `\"STDOUT Options\"` and `\"Extension Output Options\"`\n- Choice fields should be multi-select to allow combining options\n- Choice values describe content groups, for example: `\"Show Summary\"`, `\"Show Metadata\"`, `\"Show Item Details\"`, or they can be `\"Show Summary on STDOUT\"`, `\"Show Metadata on Extension Output\"`...\n\n**Key Notes:**\n- These fields control what is included in STDOUT and Extension Output \u2014 they do not affect output artifacts like files\n- Default selection should reflect the most commonly useful output to minimise noise for typical use cases\n- Keeping STDOUT and Extension Output small is important as it is stored in the UAC database\n\n---\n\n### Large Output Safety Net Pattern\n\n**When to Use:** Extension produces record-based output (e.g., query results, file listings, processed records) that may be large, and that output is written inline to STDOUT or Extension Output \n\n**Structure:**\n- Use environment variable `UE_MAX_OUTPUT_RECORDS` to cap the number of records written to STDOUT or Extension Output\n- Default to `100` records if the environment variable is not set\n- The cap applies only to inline output \u2014 it does not affect the number of records written to output files (e.g., CSV)\n- When the output is truncated, include a note in STDOUT, a warning on STDERR, a field on extension output as metadata, indicating the total record count and the applied limit\n\n**Key Notes:**\n- This pattern is a safety net to prevent large data from bloating the UAC database or exceeding Universal Agent output limits\n- For cases where large structured output is needed by downstream tasks, prefer writing to a file and returning the file path as metadata in Extension Output rather than embedding the full dataset inline\n\n---\n\n# Start Small\n\n**Start Small**\n- Do not propose more than two actions for the initial implementation\n- If multiple actions are inside the requirements then propose to implemement 2 max actions as a start.\n\n---\n\n# Field Organization Strategies\n\n**By Lifecycle (Recommended for most extensions):**\n\n```\n1. Action Selection (action_type choice)\n2. Connection Fields (host, port, credentials)\n3. Configuration Fields (resource identifiers, options)\n4. Execution Options (timeout, retry)\n5. Output Options\n6. Output Only Fieldss(result, status, created_id)\n```\n\n**By Logical Function (For complex multi-provider extensions):**\n```\n1. Provider/Protocol Selection\n2. Provider-Specific Connection\n3. Action Selection\n4. Action-Specific Parameters\n5. Global Options\n6. Output Fields\n```\n\n---\n\n## Python Modules selection framework\n\n- Python core modules provide outstanding quality, less maintenance cost and better backwards compatibility\n- Select modules that are specialized on the functional area you are operating on\n- Select modules that have proven and documented compatibility with the target python versions\n- Select modules that are very well maintained and have adequate popularity\n- It is preferable not to select a big library to use only a small fraction of it's functionality, unless it is the only option you have\n- **Platform compatibility**: When the target UAC agent platform is Linux-only, C extension modules with a confirmed `manylinux_2_17_x86_64` wheel are viable. When the target is Windows-only, cross-platform, or not stated, only pure-Python modules are safe \u2014 C extensions cannot be reliably bundled for non-Linux targets given the Linux-based build system. Prefer pure-Python alternatives where they exist and functional trade-offs are acceptable.\n \n\n## Python Modules specific Notes\n\n### HTTPX vs REQUESTS\n\n- They have different handling with respect to CA Bundles and Certificates\n- They are using different environment variables\n- Requests library is still the more commonly used one\n- HTTPX has better async operation logic\n- HTTPX supports encrypted Client Certificates, while Requests library does not.\n- HTTPX has mode fine grained control of timeouts\n\n**Comments applicable to both libraries**\n\n- Environment variables that point to a CA certificate bundle are not important in case of well known public domains. `certifi` module is sufficient for domain verification\n- Environment variables that point to a CA certificate bundle make sense in local installations (like a local service installation) and when self signed certificates come into play\n\n\n### Tabulate module (or similar ones)\n\n- They play very nicely for displaying tabular format on STDOUT\n- Use `tablefmt=\"rounded_outline\"` as it provides a realy nice output\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Extension Categories\n\nThe following categories are the most popular ones.\n\n**Web Service Based** \n- Description: The extension is calling webservice directly using `requests` or other similar low-level module to fulfil the business logic\n- Examples: \n  - Integrating with  OpenWeather API using `requests` python module library.\n\n**Python Module Based** \n- Description: The extension is calling a specialized module that helps for the fulfilment of business case \n- Examples:\n  - Integration is using pandas module to do some kind of data transformation to fulfil the business logic.\n  - Integration is using aws boto3 python module to send a message to an AWS SQS queue.\n\n**Command Execution**\n- Description: The extesion is calling an executable from the Agent Host that helps in the fulfilment of the business case. \n- Best Practice: The executable needs to exist on the Agent and should not be bundled within the extension archive.\n- Examples:\n  - Integration is calling `ls` to get a list of files.\n  - Integration is calling `aws` to interact with AWS Services\n  - Integration is calling `kubectl` to interact with a kubernetes Cluster\n\n\n## Related Documentation\n\n- **ue-template-structure.md** - High-level template structure overview\n- **ue-template-standard-fields.md** - Template-level properties\n- **ue-template-standard-extension-fields.md** - Field properties reference\n",
      "numLines": 740,
      "startLine": 1,
      "totalLines": 740
    }
